WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.250
Good morning, John. Do you know what life
is? This question has always fascinated me.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.600
I mean, at first, it seems like kind of a
simple question. Like, you're not dead, you're

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.900
alive. Pretty big difference there. When you're
dead, you're stinky, you're decomposing, you

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.770
turn--and you stop moving. It's pretty--it's
pretty clear.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.339
But if you think about it for a long time,
it's not whether or not I'm alive or dead,

00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:21.710
it's whether or not a virus is ever alive
or whether or not a computer virus is ever

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:25.369
alive. What about Johnny 5 and those little
things from Batteries Not Included? And if

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:27.500
you weren't alive in the 80s, what about GLaDOS?

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.199
If we don't have the definition, how will
we know when we've created something that's

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:36.890
alive? And does it really matter and is this
all just pseudo-intellectual navel-gazing?

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:41.000
Navel-gazing is a weird term. Staring into
your belly? Whoa. What is that?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.260
So when I asked on Twitter, I got some funny
answers, but mostly what I got was a list

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:49.290
of criteria which appears to be what is being
taught in schools right now. Move, respire,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:54.421
sense, reproduce, excrete, and nourish. First,
that is a list of criteria; it is not a definition.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:58.940
Second, that list of criteria seems a little
bit unnecessarily restrictive to me. Like,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.870
Lieutenant Commander Data? If you are a Star
Trek nerd, he doesn't respire, he doesn't

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.909
grow, he doesn't reproduce, but nonetheless,
he is alive, he's more than alive, he's sentient.

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:11.570
So my personal definition--this is the one
that I've come up with: life wants. If thing

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.899
x requires thing y in order to continue its
existence, and thing x shows attempts to acquire

00:01:16.899 --> 00:01:22.899
thing y, then thing x, apparently wanting
thing y, is alive. A side note, if wanting

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:27.130
is in fact the fundamental attribute of what
makes things alive, I think it goes a long

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.460
to explaining why humans never seem to stop
wanting.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.749
I mean, even if you got like twenty thing-a-mabobs,
you got whozits and whatzits galore, still...

00:01:35.749 --> 00:01:39.450
Oh, I'm about to embarass myself, aren't I?
We want more!

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:44.670
The problem with the definition of "want"
is that it's fairly easy to create a computer

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.299
program that wants something. Like, Google
Search Bot, for example, wants to seek out

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.060
and search information and send it back. But
does that make Google software alive?

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.310
But beyond the biological definition, my favorite
definition of life is Schrödinger's. And

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:01.139
yes, Schrödinger, the dude with the cat.
So entropy is a physical, measurable physical

00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:05.990
quantity, quality, like mass, or heat, you
know, and it's a measurement of disorganization.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.929
As your entropy goes up, you become less organized.

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:13.989
My body, right here, from the strands of DNA
in the cells of my feet to my delightfully

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:20.470
tousled mop of hair is an extremely organized
sy--really organized, just tremendous amount

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.629
of organization. But the second law of thermodynamics
states that entropy must, as a rule, increase,

00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:28.959
and the disorder of the universe will forever
increase.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:36.620
What, then, is this? This magnificent, near-perfect,
amazingly organized meat that contains me?

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:42.310
Schrödinger said basically, I'm paraphrasing,
life is any system that continuously decreases

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.920
its own entropy using energy around it. Basically,
Schrödinger took a fundamental philosophical

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.640
question and answered it with an equation.
Which is just amazing.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.950
But if life organizes itself using energy,
then Schrödinger's definition kinda is just

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:01.360
as bad as mine when it comes to Google. Though
Google no doubt was created by man, it currently,

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:07.160
on its own, organizes itself using the energy
that it has, which is the power from the power

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.160
grid.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.909
So, is Google alive? I'm going to break some
rules here and venture a cautious yes. And

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:16.950
you are welcome to comment on my idiocy in
the comments.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.840
Now there are some people who say that they
value all individual living organisms just

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.950
because they are alive. To those people I
say mehhh. You don't really believe that.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.959
I made a point this morning to not brush my
teeth.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:27.069
Yeah, how is that related?

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:31.090
But because I didn't brush my teeth today,
there are approximately a hundred billion

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.269
living organisms in my mouth right now. The
question becomes: do all those organisms have

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:40.480
value, and if they do, do I have a responsibility
not to kill all of them? Uh, the answer is

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:46.129
no, they don't have value, and I don't care
if they live or die, 'cause my breath's stinky.

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:51.000
And neither do you, at least, I hope, because
you also kill billions of them every single

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.540
day. John, I will see you on Wednesday.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.459
GLaDOS: The only thing you've managed to break
so far is my heart.

